+# 03: Expiry by the clock
+
+**What to build:** A Reservation that nobody confirms or releases expires on its own once its expiry time is reached, and its units return to Available. There is no background job and no sweep call: expiry is observed purely through the injected clock. Every read reflects it (`get_reservation`, `held`, `available`, and therefore `place_order` and new reservations). The expiry time is fixed when the Reservation is made.
+
+**Blocked by:** 01 (Reserve and look up a Reservation)
+
+**Status:** resolved
+
+- [x] One instant before `expires_at` the Reservation is ACTIVE and its units are still Held
+- [x] At exactly `expires_at` (and after) `get_reservation` reports EXPIRED, Held drops to 0 for its units and Available recovers, with no sweep or other call in between
+- [x] Expired units can be taken by a new Reservation or by `place_order`
+- [x] A custom `reservation_ttl` passed to the service changes when expiry happens
+- [x] `expires_at` never changes after the Reservation is made
+- [x] Time in tests moves only by advancing or setting the fixed clock; no patching
+
+## Comments
+
+Resolved on integration branch `stock-reservations` (tip 261fe03); all 91 tests pass.
