+# Stock reservations
+
+Status: resolved
+
+## Problem Statement
+
+Checkout has no way to set stock aside for a cart while the customer pays. Between "customer clicks pay" and "payment succeeds", the units can be taken by another order, so a paid cart may turn out to be unfulfillable. The only tool today is `place_order`, which allocates immediately and permanently (until shipped or cancelled), is all-or-nothing, and has no notion of an abandoned checkout: if the customer walks away, someone has to remember to cancel. The cart service also retries calls on timeouts, and today a retry would allocate stock twice.
+
+## Solution
+
+A **Reservation**: a time-limited hold on stock for a cart, taken before payment. The cart service reserves stock under an **idempotency key**, and the inventory holds as many of the **Requested** units as are **Available** right now. When payment succeeds, checkout **confirms** the Reservation into an ordinary placed Order for exactly the units held. If the customer abandons the cart, checkout **releases** it. If nobody does either, the Reservation **expires** on its own after 20 minutes and its units become Available again. No background job is needed: the passage of time on the injected clock is enough. Retrying a reserve with the same key and the same request returns the same Reservation instead of holding stock twice.
+
+**Available** becomes On-hand minus Allocated minus **Held**, so held units cannot be promised to anyone else, while still being distinct from the Allocated units of placed Orders.
+
+## User Stories
+
+### Reserving
+
+1. As the cart service, I want to reserve stock for a cart in a given warehouse, so that the units cannot be sold to someone else while the customer pays.
+2. As the cart service, I want a Reservation to hold as many units per line as are Available right now (up to what I requested), so that I can tell the customer "only 3 left — we've held them for you" instead of failing the whole cart.
+3. As the cart service, I want lines with nothing Available to be left out of the held items entirely (not listed with 0), so that the held items list exactly what is being held.
+4. As the cart service, I want the Reservation to also record what I requested, so that I can show the shortfall by comparing it with what was held.
+5. As the cart service, I want reserve to fail with an insufficient-stock error only when no line can hold anything, so that a partial hold is never reported as a failure.
+6. As the cart service, I want a reserve that fails for lack of stock to create nothing, so that no empty Reservation is left lying around.
+7. As the cart service, I want an unknown warehouse to be rejected, so that a typo in configuration is caught immediately.
+8. As the cart service, I want an unknown SKU to be rejected, so that I do not silently get a Reservation missing a product.
+9. As the cart service, I want an empty item list to be rejected, so that a malformed request is caught.
+10. As the cart service, I want a zero, negative, or non-integer quantity to be rejected, so that a malformed request is caught.
+11. As the cart service, I want a rejected request to hold nothing at all, not even for its valid lines, so that validation failures have no side effects.
+12. As the cart service, I want every Reservation to get a unique sequential id (`RES-0001`, `RES-0002`, …), so that I can refer to it in later calls and in logs.
+13. As the cart service, I want each Reservation to tell me when it expires, so that I can show the customer a countdown and know when I must have confirmed.
+14. As the cart service, I want each Reservation to tell me when it was made, so that I can audit checkout timing.
+15. As the cart service, I want a new Reservation to start ACTIVE with no Order, so that its state is unambiguous.
+
+### Idempotent retries
+
+16. As the cart service, I want to retry a reserve with the same idempotency key and the same request and get back the original Reservation, so that network retries never hold stock twice.
+17. As the cart service, I want such a replay to return the Reservation as it is now (active, confirmed, released or expired), so that I learn its real state rather than a stale copy.
+18. As the cart service, I want a replay to neither re-check stock nor move the expiry time, so that retrying cannot extend a hold or change what was held.
+19. As the cart service, I want "same request" to mean the same warehouse and the same requested items regardless of their order, so that re-serialising my cart does not count as a different request.
+20. As the cart service, I want "same request" to be judged against what I requested, not what ended up held, so that retrying a partially held cart with the original request is recognised as a replay.
+21. As the cart service, I want reusing a key with a different warehouse or different items to raise an idempotency conflict, so that a bug that reuses keys is caught instead of silently returning someone else's Reservation.
+22. As the cart service, I want an idempotency key to stay bound to its Reservation forever, so that a late retry never creates a second hold.
+23. As the cart service, I want a reserve that failed (validation or insufficient stock) not to use up its key, so that I can retry the same key once stock arrives.
+24. As the cart service, I want an empty or blank idempotency key to be rejected, so that callers that forget to set one do not all share a single Reservation.
+25. As the cart service, I want the idempotency lookup to happen before validation and stock checks, so that a valid replay succeeds even when stock has since run out.
+
+### Confirming
+
+26. As checkout, I want to confirm an active Reservation once payment succeeds, so that the held units become a placed Order.
+27. As checkout, I want the Order to contain exactly the units the Reservation held, so that a partially held cart becomes an Order for only what was held.
+28. As checkout, I want the confirmed Order to be an ordinary placed Order (normal `ORD-nnnn` id, placed at confirm time), so that shipping and cancelling work exactly as for any other Order.
+29. As checkout, I want confirm to mark the Reservation CONFIRMED and record the Order's id on it, so that I can trace a Reservation to its Order.
+30. As checkout, I want confirm to succeed on an active Reservation without re-checking stock, so that a write-off after the hold cannot break a paid checkout.
+31. As a warehouse manager, I want Available to stay the same across a confirm, so that confirming merely turns Held units into Allocated units.
+32. As checkout, I want to retry confirm on an already CONFIRMED Reservation and get back the same Order, at any time (even after the Reservation's expiry time), so that confirm retries are safe.
+33. As checkout, I want confirming an expired Reservation to raise a reservation-expired error, so that I know the hold is gone and must not take payment against it.
+34. As checkout, I want confirming a released Reservation to raise an invalid-reservation-state error, so that a released cart cannot be resurrected.
+
+### Releasing
+
+35. As checkout, I want to release an active Reservation when the customer abandons the cart, so that its units become Available again immediately.
+36. As checkout, I want releasing an already released Reservation to return it unchanged, so that release retries are safe.
+37. As checkout, I want releasing an expired Reservation to return it unchanged (still EXPIRED), so that I do not have to race the expiry time when cleaning up.
+38. As checkout, I want releasing a confirmed Reservation to raise an invalid-reservation-state error, so that a paid Order cannot be undone through the Reservation (cancel the Order instead).
+
+### Expiry
+
+39. As a warehouse manager, I want Reservations nobody confirms or releases to expire on their own after 20 minutes, so that abandoned carts do not lock stock forever.
+40. As an operator, I want the reservation lifetime to be configurable when constructing the service, so that it can be tuned without code changes.
+41. As an existing caller, I want the service to keep working when constructed with just repository, ledger and clock, so that nothing outside checkout has to change.
+42. As a warehouse manager, I want a Reservation to count as expired from the instant its expiry time is reached, so that the boundary is unambiguous.
+43. As a warehouse manager, I want expiry to take effect without anyone running a sweep, so that stock frees up even if no job runs.
+44. As the cart service, I want looking up an expired Reservation to report it as EXPIRED, so that I see its real state.
+45. As a warehouse manager, I want an expiry time that never moves after the Reservation is made, so that a hold cannot be stretched indefinitely.
+
+### Looking up
+
+46. As the cart service, I want to look up a Reservation by id, so that I can show its state and contents.
+47. As the cart service, I want an unknown Reservation id to raise a reservation-not-found error on lookup, confirm and release alike, so that bad ids are reported consistently.
+
+### Stock figures
+
+48. As a warehouse manager, I want to see how many units of a SKU are Held in a warehouse, so that I understand why Available is lower than On-hand minus Allocated.
+49. As a warehouse manager, I want Held to count only active, unexpired Reservations, so that confirmed, released and expired ones stop counting at once.
+50. As a warehouse manager, I want Allocated to keep meaning only units on placed Orders, so that existing reports and reasoning stay valid.
+51. As a sales channel, I want Available to subtract Held units, so that I never promise units that are on hold for a cart.
+52. As a sales channel, I want Available never to go below zero, so that write-offs below the promised amount do not produce negative availability.
+53. As a warehouse manager, I want Reservations in one warehouse not to affect stock figures in another, so that warehouses stay independent.
+54. As a warehouse manager, I want the back-office stock report to show a Held column, so that each row adds up.
+55. As a sales channel, I want `place_order` to remain all-or-nothing and to respect Held units, so that direct orders cannot take stock that is on hold.
+
+## Implementation Decisions
+
+- **Vocabulary** follows `GLOSSARY.md`: Reservation, Requested, Held, Allocated, Available, Confirm, Release, Expire, Idempotency key. ADR-0001 records why reserve holds what it can while `place_order` stays all-or-nothing; do not unify them.
+- **Models module** gains:
+  - Enum `ReservationStatus` with members `ACTIVE`, `CONFIRMED`, `RELEASED`, `EXPIRED`.
+  - Mutable dataclass `Reservation` (mutable like `Order`) with, in this order, the fields the hidden tests rely on: `reservation_id: str`, `warehouse: str`, `items: dict[str, int]` (units held per SKU; only SKUs with at least one unit held), `status: ReservationStatus`, `expires_at: datetime`, `order_id: str | None`; followed by `requested: dict[str, int]` (what the cart asked for) and `reserved_at: datetime`.
+- **Errors module** gains four subclasses of `InventoryError`, carrying context attributes in the style of the existing errors:
+  - `ReservationNotFound` (reservation id).
+  - `ReservationExpired` (reservation id, expiry time).
+  - `InvalidReservationState` (reservation id, status, attempted action), mirroring `InvalidOrderState`.
+  - `IdempotencyConflict` (idempotency key).
+  - Existing errors are reused: `UnknownWarehouse`, `UnknownSku`, `InvalidQuantity`, `InvalidOrder` (empty items, blank idempotency key), `InsufficientStock` (nothing at all can be held: names the first requested line in caller order, with its requested quantity and `available=0`).
+- **Repository** gains storage for Reservations: save, get-by-id (raising `ReservationNotFound`), listing (filterable by warehouse at least), a sequential id generator producing `RES-0001`, `RES-0002`, …, and an idempotency key index mapping key to reservation id.
+- **Service constructor** gains an optional keyword `reservation_ttl` (a `timedelta`, default 20 minutes). The positional `(repository, ledger, clock)` signature is unchanged.
+- **Service queries:**
+  - New `held(sku, warehouse)`: total units across Reservations in that warehouse that are effectively ACTIVE (stored ACTIVE and `clock.now() < expires_at`). Validates SKU and warehouse like the other queries.
+  - `allocated(sku, warehouse)`: unchanged.
+  - `available(sku, warehouse)`: `max(0, on_hand − allocated − held)`.
+- **Expiry is derived from the clock, never swept.** A Reservation stored as ACTIVE is effectively EXPIRED once `clock.now() >= expires_at`. Every public entry point (`get_reservation`, `confirm_reservation`, `release_reservation`, an idempotent replay of `reserve`, and `held`/`available`) must observe this. Whether the stored status is updated lazily on access or computed on read is up to the implementer; the observable behaviour is what matters. There is no sweep method.
+- **`reserve(warehouse, items, idempotency_key) -> Reservation`**, in this order:
+  1. A blank idempotency key (empty or whitespace only) → `InvalidOrder`.
+  2. Key already bound: if the warehouse and the requested items (dict equality, so order does not matter) match the original request, return the original Reservation in its current effective state, with no stock check and no change to `expires_at`. Otherwise → `IdempotencyConflict`.
+  3. Validate as `place_order` does: unknown warehouse, empty items, unknown SKU, invalid quantity. Any failure means nothing is held and the key is not recorded.
+  4. For each line, held = `min(requested, available now)`, and lines with 0 are omitted. If every line is 0 → `InsufficientStock` (see above), and nothing is saved or recorded.
+  5. Otherwise save a new ACTIVE Reservation with `reserved_at = now`, `expires_at = now + reservation_ttl`, `order_id = None`, and bind the key to it.
+- **`confirm_reservation(reservation_id) -> Order`**, by effective status:
+  - ACTIVE: create a PLACED Order (normal `ORD-nnnn` id from the existing sequence, `placed_at = now`, one line per held SKU with the held quantity, in the Reservation's item order). There is no stock re-check. Set the Reservation to CONFIRMED with `order_id` set. Held drops and Allocated rises by the same amounts, so Available is unchanged.
+  - CONFIRMED: return the existing Order (looked up by `order_id`) as it is now, regardless of the clock.
+  - RELEASED → `InvalidReservationState`.
+  - EXPIRED → `ReservationExpired`.
+- **`release_reservation(reservation_id) -> Reservation`**, by effective status:
+  - ACTIVE → RELEASED.
+  - RELEASED: return unchanged.
+  - EXPIRED: return unchanged, still EXPIRED.
+  - CONFIRMED → `InvalidReservationState`.
+- **`get_reservation(reservation_id) -> Reservation`**: returns the Reservation in its effective state, and raises `ReservationNotFound` for unknown ids.
+- **Orders created by confirm are ordinary Orders.** `ship_order` and `cancel_order` apply unchanged. Cancelling such an Order does not reactivate the Reservation (it stays CONFIRMED).
+- **`place_order`** is unchanged apart from checking against the new Available, so it respects Held units.
+- **Reports module:** `StockRow` gains `held: int = 0` as its last field, so existing positional constructions keep working. `stock_report` fills it from `held()`, and its `available` column reflects the new formula.
+- **Package exports** gain `Reservation` and `ReservationStatus`.
+- **README** Concepts section gains Held, the new Available formula, and the Reservation lifecycle (reserve → confirm or release, or expire).
+
+## Testing Decisions
+
+- **Good tests exercise external behaviour only:** they call public `InventoryService` methods (and `stock_report`) and assert on returned values, raised errors and stock figures. They never reach into the repository's internals (for example, idempotency is tested by calling `reserve` twice, not by inspecting the key index).
+- **Seams:** (1) `InventoryService` built exactly as in the shared fixtures, from a real `InMemoryRepository`, a real `Ledger` and a `FixedClock`; (2) `stock_report`, only for the Held column. No new seams, no mocks.
+- **Time** is driven only by advancing or setting the `FixedClock`. Expiry tests advance to just before, exactly at, and past `expires_at`.
+- **New test module** for reservations, in the style of the existing order tests (a `stocked` fixture, one behaviour per test, descriptive test names). The report test module gains a case for the Held column.
+- **Behaviours to cover at minimum:**
+  - full hold; partial hold; omitted zero line; nothing holdable → `InsufficientStock` and nothing created; validation errors hold nothing (including valid lines alongside an invalid one).
+  - Held/Available arithmetic; warehouse isolation; `place_order` cannot take Held units; `place_order` still all-or-nothing.
+  - default 20-minute TTL and a custom TTL via the constructor; three-argument construction still works.
+  - expiry boundary (active one instant before, expired at `expires_at`); expired units return to Available without any sweep; `get_reservation` reports EXPIRED.
+  - confirm: Order contains exactly the held units; status and `order_id`; Available unchanged; no re-check after a write-off; repeat confirm returns the same Order even after expiry; expired → `ReservationExpired`; released → `InvalidReservationState`; confirmed Order ships and cancels normally.
+  - release: frees units; repeat release is a no-op; release of expired returns it EXPIRED; release of confirmed → `InvalidReservationState`.
+  - idempotency: replay returns same id with current status; replay does not re-check stock or move `expires_at`; item-order-insensitive match; match is judged on Requested, not held; conflicting warehouse or items → `IdempotencyConflict`; failed reserve leaves key reusable; blank key → `InvalidOrder`.
+  - unknown id → `ReservationNotFound` for get, confirm and release.
+  - sequential `RES-nnnn` ids.
+- **Prior art:** the existing order tests (lifecycle and state errors), stock tests (validation errors), report tests (`StockRow` equality), and the shared fixtures for construction.
+- The existing suite must keep passing unchanged.
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Extending or refreshing a Reservation's expiry. The cart releases and reserves again under a new key.
+- Per-warehouse or per-SKU reservation lifetimes.
+- Any background sweep, scheduler or explicit expire call.
+- Partially confirming or partially releasing a Reservation, or editing its items.
+- Reactivating a Reservation when its Order is cancelled, and any link from Order back to Reservation beyond `order_id` on the Reservation.
+- Expiring or garbage-collecting idempotency keys.
+- Changing `place_order` to hold partially.
+- Persistence beyond the in-memory repository, and concurrency control.
+
+## Further Notes
+
+- Write-offs can leave On-hand below Allocated plus Held. That is accepted (Available floors at zero), and confirm still succeeds, consistent with how placed Orders already behave after write-offs.
+- Hidden tests call exactly the interface named above (`reserve`, `confirm_reservation`, `release_reservation`, `get_reservation`, the `Reservation` fields and `ReservationStatus` members, and the four new errors). Keep those names and signatures exact.
